<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#manuscript-illumination">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manuscript illumination</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">Manuscript</a> illumination is the decoration of handwritten books with painted images, ornament and gold. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>, Persian illumination and painting absorbed Chinese influences, producing works such as illustrated histories and copies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahnameh">Shahnameh</a>. This art laid foundations for the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-miniature">Persian miniature</a> tradition.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manuscript-illumination.jpg" alt="Manuscript illumination" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benedictine monastery of Podlažice · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sapientia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>An illuminated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> is a formally prepared document whose text is decorated with borders, miniature illustrations and other flourishes. The earliest to survive are a small number from late antiquity, dating from between 400 and 600 CE, among them the Vergilius Romanus, the Vergilius Vaticanus and the Rossano Gospels. Most surviving examples come from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, and many thousands survive. Islamic manuscripts can also be called illuminated and use essentially the same techniques, but comparable works from the Far East and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a> are described as painted.</p><p>The first known decorated Arabic manuscripts are some Qur'ans of the 9th century, with decorated frontispieces and headings but no illustrations. Illustrated Arabic manuscripts grew from the Graeco-Arabic translation movement, and production greatly increased in the 12th and especially the 13th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-miniature">Persian miniature</a> tradition began mostly in whole books, and the Great Mongol Shahnameh, probably from the 1330s, is a very early manuscript of a work that became a favourite for grand illustrated books in Persian courts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Under the strictest definition a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> is not illuminated unless it contains metal, normally gold leaf or shell gold paint, which from the 12th century was usually burnished. Gold exalted a religious text and displayed a patron's wealth, until it became so common that it made up only a tenth of the cost of production.</p><p>Most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> were written on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parchment">parchment</a>, or on vellum from calf skin, and bound into codices. The text was usually written first with space reserved for decoration. A rubricator added titles and initials in red, and then an illuminator drew the design, applied gold and painted the colours. A Gothic page might hold a framed miniature, a historiated initial and a border with drolleries, often by different artists.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Illumination served prayers and liturgical books such as psalters, and from the 13th century also secular texts. Up to the 12th century most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> were produced in monastic scriptoria, and by the 14th century commercial workshops in cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> did much of the work. Wealthy patrons commissioned books of hours, with calendars of their own saints, and the Très Riches Heures du Duc de Berry is one of the best known. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">Printing</a> rapidly led to the decline of illumination, and in the early 16th century books were still made but in much smaller numbers, mostly for the very wealthy.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Illuminated manuscript” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ilkhanid culture blended Mongol, Persian and Chinese traditions, particularly in <b>manuscript illumination</b> and architecture, as in the great domed mausoleum at Sultaniyya built for Öljeitü, completed around 1313.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
